INT. COUNTY HOSPITAL - NIGHT WARD - NIGHT

A long ward at lights-low. A dozen iron beds, white curtains drawn on half. A ceiling fan turns slowly. A clock above the door reads 3:10.

DR. WINIFRED OSEI (40s, white coat, stethoscope slung like a scarf) sits on the edge of an empty bed, shoes off, rubbing her arch. NURSE CARMELA BRUNO (50s, starched cap, cardigan over her uniform) crosses the aisle with a tray of paper cups, sets one down beside the doctor, pours her own, and sits on the next bed over; she lifts her feet onto the rail, Winifred leans back on her elbows, and the two women sit in the pool of lamplight like old friends on a porch.

WINIFRED
Bed nine?

CARMELA
Asleep. Finally.

WINIFRED
Good.

CARMELA
She asked for you.

WINIFRED
She asks for everyone.

CARMELA
She asked for you twice.

Winifred sips the tea. She makes a face.

WINIFRED
What is this?

CARMELA
Chamomile.

WINIFRED
It tastes like a hay bale.

CARMELA
It's good for you.

They both look down the ward. A patient coughs behind a curtain and settles. The fan ticks. Carmela raises her paper cup an inch. Winifred taps it with hers.
